# TypeScript Philosophy

TypeScript is mandatory across the entire codebase.

The project values:
- Strong typing
- Predictable APIs
- Readability
- Maintainability
- Developer experience

Avoid:
- Weak typing
- Unsafe patterns
- Overly complex generic abstractions
- Excessive type indirection

# Strict Typing Rules

- Always use TypeScript strict mode
- Never use any
- Avoid unknown unless necessary
- Avoid unsafe type assertions
- Explicitly type component props
- Explicitly type non-trivial function returns
- Prefer inferred types when obvious

Bad:

const data: any = response

Good:

const data: User = response

# type vs interface

Prefer type by default.

Use interface only when:
- Extending object contracts
- Public extensible APIs require merging behavior

Good:

type User = {
  id: string
  name: string
}

Avoid unnecessary interfaces.

# Naming Conventions

## Components

Use PascalCase.

Good:

UserCard
JobDetailsScreen

## Variables and Functions

Use camelCase.

Good:

fetchUserProfile
isLoading

## Constants

Use UPPER_SNAKE_CASE.

Good:

MAX_RETRY_COUNT
DEFAULT_RADIUS_KM

# File Naming

Use kebab-case for files.

Good:

user-card.tsx
create-job.schema.ts
use-auth-session.ts

Avoid:

UserCard.tsx
CreateJobSchema.ts

# Component Props

Always explicitly type props.

Good:

type Props = {
  title: string
  onPress: () => void
}

export function Button(props: Props) {}

Avoid implicit props.

# Function Rules

- Keep functions small
- Prefer pure functions
- Avoid hidden side effects
- Prefer early returns
- Prefer explicit behavior
- Avoid deeply nested conditions

Bad:

if (user) {
  if (user.profile) {
    if (user.profile.avatar) {
    }
  }
}

Better:

if (!user?.profile?.avatar) {
  return
}

# Optional Values

Use optional chaining and nullish coalescing when appropriate.

Good:

const avatar = user?.avatarUrl ?? defaultAvatar

# Enums

Prefer union types over enums when possible.

Good:

type JobStatus =
  | 'pending'
  | 'accepted'
  | 'completed'

Avoid unnecessary enums.

# Reusable Types

Shared reusable types belong in:

types/

Feature-specific types belong inside feature modules.

Good:

features/jobs/types/job.ts

# API Types

Always type:
- API responses
- mutations
- repository calls
- hook returns
- form schemas

Avoid untyped backend communication.

# Zod Integration

Prefer deriving types from Zod schemas.

Good:

const schema = z.object({
  title: z.string(),
})

type FormData = z.infer<typeof schema>

# Generic Rules

Avoid overly abstract generic systems.

Prefer:
- readability
- explicitness
- maintainability

Over:
- advanced type wizardry
- unnecessary abstractions

# Async Rules

- Always handle async errors
- Never ignore rejected promises
- Prefer async/await over chained promises

Good:

try {
  await createJob()
} catch (error) {
  handleError(error)
}

# Imports

Prefer absolute imports when configured.

Keep imports organized:
1. external libraries
2. shared modules
3. feature modules
4. relative imports

# Maintainability Standards

Always prioritize:
- readable code
- explicit intent
- predictable behavior
- simple APIs

Avoid:
- magic behavior
- hidden mutations
- clever abstractions
- excessive indirection

# Cursor AI Expectations

When generating TypeScript:
- Generate strict-safe code
- Avoid any
- Prefer readable types
- Respect existing naming conventions
- Prefer maintainable solutions
- Keep APIs simple and explicit